## 3. Adaptive difficulty probing

Do not keep testing on tasks both parent and child already solve.

The Progress Oracle should probe the frontier.

```python
def frontier_duel(parent, child, generator, budget):
    posterior = AbilityPosterior()

    difficulty = posterior.estimated_parent_frontier()

    for _ in range(budget.rounds):
        tasks = [
            generator.sample(rng, difficulty=difficulty, tags=budget.tags)
            for _ in range(budget.batch_size)
        ]

        parent_runs = run(parent, tasks)
        child_runs = run(child, tasks)

        comparisons = compare_outcomes(parent_runs, child_runs)
        posterior.update(comparisons)

        difficulty = posterior.next_most_informative_difficulty()

    return posterior.delta()
```

A simple version works immediately:

```python
delta_i = child_outcome_i.quality - parent_outcome_i.quality
quality_delta_lcb = empirical_bernstein_lcb(delta_i)
```

A better version uses an ability model:

```text
P(success | runtime, task)
    = sigmoid(runtime_ability - task_difficulty)
```

Then estimate:

```text
θ_child - θ_parent
```

Promote only if:

```text
LCB(θ_child - θ_parent) > δ_capability
```

This gives you a continuous notion of “better” even when individual tasks are pass/fail.

---

## 4. Pairwise blind artifact comparison

For open-ended tasks, do not ask “what score is this answer?”

Ask:

```text
Given the same task, is artifact B better than artifact A on axis X?
```

The comparator sees:

```text
task prompt
rubric axis
artifact A
artifact B
```

It does not see:

```text
which artifact came from parent
which artifact came from child
trace length
model name
runtime topology
cost
latency
mutation details
```

The result is:

```python
class AxisDuel(BaseModel):
    axis_id: str
    verdict: Literal[
        "a_better",
        "b_better",
        "tie",
        "incomparable",
        "abstain",
    ]
    margin: float
    confidence: float
    concrete_reasons: list[str]
    checkable_subclaims: list[str]
```

The child receives a win only after order randomization is decoded.

For example:

```text
Axis: actionability

A gives vague advice.
B gives a concrete sequence of next actions, names assumptions,
and flags one missing input.

Verdict: B better
```

But this is not enough by itself. The comparator must be calibrated.

So each judge has a calibration record:

```python
class ComparatorCalibration(BaseModel):
    comparator_id: str
    axis_id: str
    agreement_with_human: float
    order_flip_consistency: float
    tie_calibration_error: float
    length_bias: float
    authority: AuthorityLevel
```

A model judge alone can contribute weak evidence. A human preference label from the target user is high-authority for preference, though not necessarily for factual correctness.

That distinction matters.

