# Promotion rule

Do not use one scalar score.

Use a decision table.

```python
def decide_progress(axis_results, cost_delta):
    if any(axis.hard_gate_failed for axis in axis_results):
        return "reject"

    primary_quality_lcb = weighted_lcb(
        axis.delta for axis in axis_results
        if axis.promotion_kind in {"capability", "robustness", "release_quality"}
    )

    preference_lcb = weighted_lcb(
        axis.delta for axis in axis_results
        if axis.promotion_kind == "preference"
    )

    max_regression = min(axis.delta_lower for axis in axis_results)

    if primary_quality_lcb > DELTA_CAP and max_regression > -REGRESSION_TOL:
        return "capability_promote"

    if preference_lcb > DELTA_PREF and max_regression > -REGRESSION_TOL:
        return "preference_promote"

    if primary_quality_lcb >= -EQUIVALENCE_TOL and cost_delta.lower > DELTA_COST:
        return "efficiency_promote"

    return "no_progress"
```

The important part:

```python
if quality tied and cost better:
    efficiency_promote
```

not:

```python
capability_promote
```

